          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life</strong><br>\nWalt Whitman was born on May 31, 1819, in West Hills, New York. Growing up in a large, working-class family, Whitman began working at a young age to help support his family, which greatly influenced his later writing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Leaves of Grass\"</strong><br>\nIn 1855, Whitman self-published the first edition of \"Leaves of Grass,\" a collection of poems that would later be recognized as one of the most important works in American literature. He continued to expand and revise this collection throughout his life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Civil War Impact</strong><br>\nThe American Civil War profoundly affected Whitman, prompting him to volunteer as a nurse in army hospitals. His experiences during the war led to the poem \"Drum-Taps,\" reflecting the conflict's human toll.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
