            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Life and Relationships</strong><br>\nWhitman never married and had no children. He formed close relationships with several men throughout his life, which, coupled with his poetry, has led to speculation about his sexuality.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Whitman as a Journalist and Editor</strong><br>\nBefore his fame as a poet, Whitman worked as a journalist and editor for several newspapers. He brought a unique and reformist perspective to the journalism of his time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nWhitman died on March 26, 1892. His work has since inspired a wide range of writers and artists, and he is often called the father of free verse, having paved the way for modern American poetry.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
